(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("troubleshooting-scenarios",{"troubleshooting-scenarios/a-symptom-first-method-narrow-the-layer-in-ten-minutes":{"html":"\u003cp>Experts do not guess faster. They shrink the search space with cheap tests and keep notes so that nothing is repeated. This lesson is the method that the following scenarios apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing the symptom precisely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Asking what changed, and where to look for it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bisecting the request path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One hypothesis, one test, one note\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Knowing when to stop and mitigate\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"state-the-symptom\">State the symptom\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write one sentence that says who sees what, since when, how often and how bad. Compare it with a normal case. Slow for everyone since 14:05 is a different problem from failing for one customer on one endpoint. Avoid causes in the sentence. A precise symptom already removes half of the possibilities.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-changed\">What changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration or flag change, a certificate or key expiry, a traffic shift, a dependency update or a quota limit. Check the deployment history, change calendar, cloud audit log and the dependency status pages around the start time. If nothing changed, look for growth: data volume, traffic or a counter that reached its limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bisect-the-path\">Bisect the path\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A request travels through client, DNS, network, load balancer, application, dependencies and storage. Test at the middle. If a request made directly to the application works, the problem lies before it. If not, it lies in or after it. Halve again. Each test removes half of the path, so a long chain needs only a few steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-hypothesis-at-a-time\">One hypothesis at a time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>State a hypothesis that a test could disprove, such as if the pool is exhausted then waiting connections will be at the maximum. Run the cheapest test, and write the result. Changing several things at once makes the outcome impossible to interpret. Prefer read only checks first, then reversible changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mitigate-first\">Mitigate first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If users are hurt, restore service before finding the root cause: roll back, fail over, scale out or disable a feature. Keep the evidence, such as logs and a heap dump, before restarting. Then continue to the cause. Hand over clearly if you get tired, with the notes you kept.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;chg;chg&gt;bis;bis&gt;mit\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A symptom-first method: narrow the layer in ten minutes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Precise symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chg\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What changed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bis\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bisect the path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mit\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mitigate and record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Precise symptom to What changed; 2, What changed to Bisect the path; 3, Bisect the path to Mitigate and record.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Precise symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who, what, when.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chg\">\u003cdt>What changed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Audit and deploys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bis\">\u003cdt>Bisect the path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Halve each time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Random.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mit\">\u003cdt>Mitigate and record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Restore then learn.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost notes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-twenty-minutes-of-guessing\">Incident: Twenty minutes of guessing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An error rate rose and three engineers tried different ideas at the same time.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout history deploy/checkout -n shop | tail -2\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deploy/checkout -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41   &lt;none&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">42   &lt;none&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2m  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  deployment/checkout  Scaled up replica set checkout-7d9 to 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each engineer changed a different setting, so no one knew which change had any effect. The incident lead stopped the changes and asked what changed. A deployment had been made six minutes before the alert, so they rolled it back and the errors stopped. The root cause, a bad configuration value, was found afterwards in a calm review with the saved logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Several people changing things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the what changed question\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guessing a cause before writing the symptom\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting before saving evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not recording the tests and results\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"state-the-symptom","text":"State the symptom"},{"id":"what-changed","text":"What changed"},{"id":"bisect-the-path","text":"Bisect the path"},{"id":"one-hypothesis-at-a-time","text":"One hypothesis at a time"},{"id":"mitigate-first","text":"Mitigate first"},{"id":"incident-twenty-minutes-of-guessing","text":"Incident: Twenty minutes of guessing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE Workbook: debugging","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/effective-troubleshooting/"},{"t":"The USE method","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry traces","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"}],"text":"Experts do not guess faster. They shrink the search space with cheap tests and keep notes so that nothing is repeated. This lesson is the method that the following scenarios apply. What you will learn Writing the symptom precisely Asking what changed, and where to look for it Bisecting the request path One hypothesis, one test, one note Knowing when to stop and mitigate State the symptom Write one sentence that says who sees what, since when, how often and how bad. Compare it with a normal case. Slow for everyone since 14:05 is a different problem from failing for one customer on one endpoint. Avoid causes in the sentence. A precise symptom already removes half of the possibilities. What changed Most incidents follow a change: a deployment, a configuration or flag change, a certificate or key expiry, a traffic shift, a dependency update or a quota limit. Check the deployment history, change calendar, cloud audit log and the dependency status pages around the start time. If nothing changed, look for growth: data volume, traffic or a counter that reached its limit. Bisect the path A request travels through client, DNS, network, load balancer, application, dependencies and storage. Test at the middle. If a request made directly to the application works, the problem lies before it. If not, it lies in or after it. Halve again. Each test removes half of the path, so a long chain needs only a few steps. One hypothesis at a time State a hypothesis that a test could disprove, such as if the pool is exhausted then waiting connections will be at the maximum. Run the cheapest test, and write the result. Changing several things at once makes the outcome impossible to interpret. Prefer read only checks first, then reversible changes. Mitigate first If users are hurt, restore service before finding the root cause: roll back, fail over, scale out or disable a feature. Keep the evidence, such as logs and a heap dump, before restarting. Then continue to the cause. Hand over clearly if you get tired, with the notes you kept. A symptom-first method: narrow the layer in ten minutes Flow in order: 1, Precise symptom to What changed; 2, What changed to Bisect the path; 3, Bisect the path to Mitigate and record. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Precise symptom Observability What it does: Who, what, when. If it fails or is weak: Vague. What changed Observability What it does: Audit and deploys. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Bisect the path Step What it does: Halve each time. If it fails or is weak: Random. Mitigate and record Step What it does: Restore then learn. If it fails or is weak: Lost notes. Incident: Twenty minutes of guessing Incident An error rate rose and three engineers tried different ideas at the same time. $ kubectl rollout history deploy/checkout -n shop | tail -2 41 \u003cnone> 42 \u003cnone> $ kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -2 2m Normal ScalingReplicaSet deployment/checkout Scaled up replica set checkout-7d9 to 6 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Each engineer changed a different setting, so no one knew which change had any effect. The incident lead stopped the changes and asked what changed. A deployment had been made six minutes before the alert, so they rolled it back and the errors stopped. The root cause, a bad configuration value, was found afterwards in a calm review with the saved logs. Common mistakes Several people changing things at once Skipping the what changed question Guessing a cause before writing the symptom Restarting before saving evidence Not recording the tests and results"},"troubleshooting-scenarios/scenario-pods-that-will-not-start-pending-image-pull-and-crash-loops":{"html":"\u003cp>A pod can fail before it is placed, before the image arrives or after the process starts. Each state points to a different owner, so the status tells you where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pending: reading scheduling events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ImagePullBackOff and ErrImagePull\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CrashLoopBackOff: exit codes and previous logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Probes that kill healthy pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A decision table from status to next command\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pending\">Pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Pending pod has been accepted but not placed or started. Run describe and read the events. The message names the cause: insufficient CPU or memory, no node matching a selector or affinity, taints without a toleration, an unbound volume claim or a quota. Fix the request, add capacity, correct the rule or bind the volume.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"image-pull-errors\">Image pull errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>ErrImagePull means the node could not fetch the image, and ImagePullBackOff means it is waiting before retrying. The events show the registry message: not found for a wrong name or tag, unauthorized for missing pull credentials, or a timeout for a network or rate limit problem. Test with the same credentials from the node or with a debug pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"crash-loops\">Crash loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>CrashLoopBackOff means the container starts and exits repeatedly. Read the last state in describe for the exit code and reason. Code 1 is an application error, 137 is a kill signal that often means out of memory, and 143 is a normal termination. Use logs with the previous flag to read the output of the crashed run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probes\">Probes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A liveness probe that fails restarts the container. A probe that is too strict, has too short a timeout or starts before the application is ready causes restarts that look like crashes. A readiness probe removes the pod from service without restarting it. Use a startup probe for slow starting applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decision-table\">Decision table\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pending leads to describe events. ImagePull leads to the registry message and credentials. CrashLoop leads to the exit code and previous logs. Running but not ready leads to the readiness probe and its endpoint. Write the status next to the command in your runbook, so that it can be followed under pressure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pend&gt;img;img&gt;crash;crash&gt;ready\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scenario: pods that will not start: Pending, image pull and crash loops\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pend\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pending\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"img\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Image pull\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"crash\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Crash loop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ready\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ready pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pending to Image pull; 2, Image pull to Crash loop; 3, Crash loop to Ready pod.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pend\">\u003cdt>Pending \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scheduling events.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Resources.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"img\">\u003cdt>Image pull \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Registry message.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"crash\">\u003cdt>Crash loop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exit code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Probe or OOM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ready\">\u003cdt>Ready pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Probe fails.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-deployment-that-kept-restarting\">Incident: The deployment that kept restarting\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new version restarted every minute and the logs were empty after each restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 -n shop | grep -A6 &quot;Last State&quot;\nkubectl logs orders-6f7 -n shop --previous | tail -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 -n shop | grep -A6 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Last State&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Last State:     Terminated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Reason:       OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Exit Code:    137\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs orders-6f7 -n shop --previous | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">starting cache warm up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The previous logs showed that the container was loading a large cache at start. The pod had a memory limit lower than the cache size, so the kernel killed it each time, and exit code 137 with OOMKilled confirmed it. The team raised the memory limit, made the warm up lazy and added a startup probe so that slow starts were not treated as failures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading current logs of a restarted container only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the events section of describe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Liveness probes without start up allowance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting limits below real peak use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying the same image tag blindly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pending","text":"Pending"},{"id":"image-pull-errors","text":"Image pull errors"},{"id":"crash-loops","text":"Crash loops"},{"id":"probes","text":"Probes"},{"id":"decision-table","text":"Decision table"},{"id":"incident-the-deployment-that-kept-restarting","text":"Incident: The deployment that kept restarting"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Debug pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/"},{"t":"Debug running pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-running-pod/"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry traces","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"}],"text":"A pod can fail before it is placed, before the image arrives or after the process starts. Each state points to a different owner, so the status tells you where to look. What you will learn Pending: reading scheduling events ImagePullBackOff and ErrImagePull CrashLoopBackOff: exit codes and previous logs Probes that kill healthy pods A decision table from status to next command Pending A Pending pod has been accepted but not placed or started. Run describe and read the events. The message names the cause: insufficient CPU or memory, no node matching a selector or affinity, taints without a toleration, an unbound volume claim or a quota. Fix the request, add capacity, correct the rule or bind the volume. Image pull errors ErrImagePull means the node could not fetch the image, and ImagePullBackOff means it is waiting before retrying. The events show the registry message: not found for a wrong name or tag, unauthorized for missing pull credentials, or a timeout for a network or rate limit problem. Test with the same credentials from the node or with a debug pod. Crash loops CrashLoopBackOff means the container starts and exits repeatedly. Read the last state in describe for the exit code and reason. Code 1 is an application error, 137 is a kill signal that often means out of memory, and 143 is a normal termination. Use logs with the previous flag to read the output of the crashed run. Probes A liveness probe that fails restarts the container. A probe that is too strict, has too short a timeout or starts before the application is ready causes restarts that look like crashes. A readiness probe removes the pod from service without restarting it. Use a startup probe for slow starting applications. Decision table Pending leads to describe events. ImagePull leads to the registry message and credentials. CrashLoop leads to the exit code and previous logs. Running but not ready leads to the readiness probe and its endpoint. Write the status next to the command in your runbook, so that it can be followed under pressure. Scenario: pods that will not start: Pending, image pull and crash loops Flow in order: 1, Pending to Image pull; 2, Image pull to Crash loop; 3, Crash loop to Ready pod. Step Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pending Step What it does: Scheduling events. If it fails or is weak: Resources. Image pull Outside party What it does: Registry message. If it fails or is weak: Credentials. Crash loop Service What it does: Exit code. If it fails or is weak: Probe or OOM. Ready pod Service What it does: Serves traffic. If it fails or is weak: Probe fails. Incident: The deployment that kept restarting Incident A new version restarted every minute and the logs were empty after each restart. $ kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 -n shop | grep -A6 \"Last State\" Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled Exit Code: 137 $ kubectl logs orders-6f7 -n shop --previous | tail -1 starting cache warm up (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The previous logs showed that the container was loading a large cache at start. The pod had a memory limit lower than the cache size, so the kernel killed it each time, and exit code 137 with OOMKilled confirmed it. The team raised the memory limit, made the warm up lazy and added a startup probe so that slow starts were not treated as failures. Common mistakes Reading current logs of a restarted container only Ignoring the events section of describe Liveness probes without start up allowance Setting limits below real peak use Retrying the same image tag blindly"},"troubleshooting-scenarios/scenario-dns-and-service-discovery-failures":{"html":"\u003cp>It is always DNS is a joke because it is often true. A slow or failing lookup looks like a slow or failing application, so test resolution early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How a name is resolved in a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Search domains, ndots and extra queries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CoreDNS: health, scaling and upstreams\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Intermittent failures and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Private zones and split horizon in clouds\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resolution-in-a-pod\">Resolution in a pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pod uses the resolver settings in resolv.conf, which point to the cluster DNS service and list search domains. A short name such as db is tried with each search suffix before the name is tried as it is. The first answer is used, and the results may be cached by the client or by a node level cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"search-domains-and-ndots\">Search domains and ndots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The ndots option says how many dots a name needs before it is tried as absolute. With the default of five, a name such as api.example.com is first tried with several cluster suffixes, producing several failed queries per lookup. This adds latency and load. Use a trailing dot for external names, or lower ndots for such workloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coredns\">CoreDNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>CoreDNS serves cluster names and forwards others to upstream resolvers. Watch its request rate, error codes, latency and cache hit rate. Scale it with the cluster, run a node local cache to cut cross node traffic and make sure that the upstream is reachable from the nodes. A crash loop in CoreDNS breaks every service that resolves names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"intermittent-failures\">Intermittent failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Occasional five second delays can come from lost UDP packets, connection tracking races on busy nodes, or a single bad upstream among several. Check the packet counters and conntrack insert failures, test with tcp and udp, and query each upstream directly. A node local cache or using tcp for retries often reduces the problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-zones\">Private zones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In clouds a private zone returns internal addresses only to linked networks. A query from an unlinked network, or from a pod that forwards to a public resolver, gets the public answer or none. Check which resolver the workload actually uses, and whether the zone is linked and has a rule for the right network.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;cache;cache&gt;core;core&gt;up\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scenario: DNS and service discovery failures\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod resolver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"core\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CoreDNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"up\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upstream and zones\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod resolver to Node cache; 2, Node cache to CoreDNS; 3, CoreDNS to Upstream and zones.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod resolver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Search and ndots.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Extra queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Node cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Local answers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"core\">\u003cdt>CoreDNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cluster names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"up\">\u003cdt>Upstream and zones \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> External names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong view.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-slow-calls-to-a-payment-provider\">Incident: Slow calls to a payment provider\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Calls to an external API took five seconds in about one case in twenty.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec deploy/checkout -n shop -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\nkubectl exec deploy/checkout -n shop -- sh -c &quot;time getent hosts api.partner.example&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/checkout -n shop -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/resolv.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nameserver 10.96.0.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">options ndots:5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/checkout -n shop -- sh -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;time getent hosts api.partner.example&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">203.0.113.20 api.partner.example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real 0m5.010s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each lookup of the external name first tried the cluster suffixes, and some of those queries were lost on a busy node, so the resolver waited for its five second timeout before retrying. The team lowered ndots for that workload, used a fully qualified name, enabled a node local cache and added an alert on the CoreDNS latency. The slow calls disappeared.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Not testing DNS first in a slow service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default ndots for workloads that call external names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Under sized CoreDNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring packet loss on a busy node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming the private zone is linked\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"resolution-in-a-pod","text":"Resolution in a pod"},{"id":"search-domains-and-ndots","text":"Search domains and ndots"},{"id":"coredns","text":"CoreDNS"},{"id":"intermittent-failures","text":"Intermittent failures"},{"id":"private-zones","text":"Private zones"},{"id":"incident-slow-calls-to-a-payment-provider","text":"Incident: Slow calls to a payment provider"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Debugging DNS resolution","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/dns-debugging-resolution/"},{"t":"DNS for services and pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/"},{"t":"Debug pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/"}],"text":"It is always DNS is a joke because it is often true. A slow or failing lookup looks like a slow or failing application, so test resolution early. What you will learn How a name is resolved in a pod Search domains, ndots and extra queries CoreDNS: health, scaling and upstreams Intermittent failures and timeouts Private zones and split horizon in clouds Resolution in a pod A pod uses the resolver settings in resolv.conf, which point to the cluster DNS service and list search domains. A short name such as db is tried with each search suffix before the name is tried as it is. The first answer is used, and the results may be cached by the client or by a node level cache. Search domains and ndots The ndots option says how many dots a name needs before it is tried as absolute. With the default of five, a name such as api.example.com is first tried with several cluster suffixes, producing several failed queries per lookup. This adds latency and load. Use a trailing dot for external names, or lower ndots for such workloads. CoreDNS CoreDNS serves cluster names and forwards others to upstream resolvers. Watch its request rate, error codes, latency and cache hit rate. Scale it with the cluster, run a node local cache to cut cross node traffic and make sure that the upstream is reachable from the nodes. A crash loop in CoreDNS breaks every service that resolves names. Intermittent failures Occasional five second delays can come from lost UDP packets, connection tracking races on busy nodes, or a single bad upstream among several. Check the packet counters and conntrack insert failures, test with tcp and udp, and query each upstream directly. A node local cache or using tcp for retries often reduces the problem. Private zones In clouds a private zone returns internal addresses only to linked networks. A query from an unlinked network, or from a pod that forwards to a public resolver, gets the public answer or none. Check which resolver the workload actually uses, and whether the zone is linked and has a rule for the right network. Scenario: DNS and service discovery failures Flow in order: 1, Pod resolver to Node cache; 2, Node cache to CoreDNS; 3, CoreDNS to Upstream and zones. Service Data store Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod resolver Service What it does: Search and ndots. If it fails or is weak: Extra queries. Node cache Data store What it does: Local answers. If it fails or is weak: Stale. CoreDNS Service What it does: Cluster names. If it fails or is weak: Overload. Upstream and zones Outside party What it does: External names. If it fails or is weak: Wrong view. Incident: Slow calls to a payment provider Incident Calls to an external API took five seconds in about one case in twenty. $ kubectl exec deploy/checkout -n shop -- cat /etc/resolv.conf search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local nameserver 10.96.0.10 options ndots:5 $ kubectl exec deploy/checkout -n shop -- sh -c \"time getent hosts api.partner.example\" 203.0.113.20 api.partner.example real 0m5.010s (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Each lookup of the external name first tried the cluster suffixes, and some of those queries were lost on a busy node, so the resolver waited for its five second timeout before retrying. The team lowered ndots for that workload, used a fully qualified name, enabled a node local cache and added an alert on the CoreDNS latency. The slow calls disappeared. Common mistakes Not testing DNS first in a slow service Default ndots for workloads that call external names Under sized CoreDNS Ignoring packet loss on a busy node Assuming the private zone is linked"},"troubleshooting-scenarios/scenario-intermittent-502-503-and-504-behind-a-load-balancer":{"html":"\u003cp>A few percent of failed requests are harder to find than a total outage. The status code and who produced it tell you which hop is unhappy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What 502, 503 and 504 each suggest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Who generated the error: client, balancer or backend\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep alive and idle timeout mismatches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks, draining and deployments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity: connection limits and queues\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-codes\">The codes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A 502 means the balancer got an invalid or no response from a backend, often because a connection was closed. A 503 means no healthy backend was available or the service was overloaded and refused work. A 504 means the backend did not answer within the timeout. Treat the code as a pointer, not as proof.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-produced-it\">Who produced it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read the balancer access log fields that show the balancer status, the backend status and the timings. If the balancer status is 502 and the backend status is empty, the backend never answered. If both are present, the backend itself returned the error. Logs on both sides, joined by a request id, remove the guesswork.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-alive\">Keep alive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the balancer reuses a connection that the backend has already closed because of its own idle timeout, the next request fails with a 502 at random. The rule is that the backend idle timeout must be longer than the balancer idle timeout. Check both settings and make the application server follow the rule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-and-draining\">Health and draining\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>During a deployment, a pod that is stopped while still in the target list receives requests that fail. Use readiness checks, a preStop delay so that the endpoint is removed first, and connection draining on the balancer. Ask whether the errors line up with deployment times.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A backend that reaches its worker or connection limit queues requests, and the balancer times out. Look at saturation: active connections, thread pool use and queue length. Scale out, add a limit that fails fast with a clear 503 or find the slow dependency that holds the workers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cl&gt;lb;lb&gt;be;be&gt;dep\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scenario: intermittent 502, 503 and 504 behind a load balancer\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"be\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dep\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dependency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Backend pods; 3, Backend pods to Dependency.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Request id.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Idle timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reuse closed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"be\">\u003cdt>Backend pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keep alive.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Closes early.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dep\">\u003cdt>Dependency \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Slow call.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Holds workers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-random-502s-every-few-minutes\">Incident: Random 502s every few minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>About half a percent of requests returned 502 with no pattern in the application logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep ' 502 ' lb-access.log | head -1\ngrep -E &quot;keepalive_timeout|idle_timeout&quot; app.conf lb.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; 502 &#x27;\u003c/span> lb-access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-11-12T10:14:03Z lb-1 502 - 0.001 backend=10.2.4.17:8080 error=connection closed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;keepalive_timeout|idle_timeout&quot;\u003c/span> app.conf lb.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app.conf: keepalive_timeout 5s;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lb.conf:  idle_timeout 60s;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application server closed idle connections after five seconds, while the balancer kept them for sixty. Whenever a request was sent on a connection that had just been closed, the balancer logged a 502 with no backend status. The team set the application keep alive to seventy five seconds, longer than the balancer, and the errors stopped. They added the rule to the service template.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Backend idle timeout shorter than the balancer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping pods before they leave the target list\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No request id across hops\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying without limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating every 5xx as an application bug\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-codes","text":"The codes"},{"id":"who-produced-it","text":"Who produced it"},{"id":"keep-alive","text":"Keep alive"},{"id":"health-and-draining","text":"Health and draining"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"incident-random-502s-every-few-minutes","text":"Incident: Random 502s every few minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Application Load Balancer troubleshooting","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/load-balancer-troubleshooting.html"},{"t":"NGINX proxy module","u":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html"},{"t":"SRE Workbook: debugging","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/effective-troubleshooting/"}],"text":"A few percent of failed requests are harder to find than a total outage. The status code and who produced it tell you which hop is unhappy. What you will learn What 502, 503 and 504 each suggest Who generated the error: client, balancer or backend Keep alive and idle timeout mismatches Health checks, draining and deployments Capacity: connection limits and queues The codes A 502 means the balancer got an invalid or no response from a backend, often because a connection was closed. A 503 means no healthy backend was available or the service was overloaded and refused work. A 504 means the backend did not answer within the timeout. Treat the code as a pointer, not as proof. Who produced it Read the balancer access log fields that show the balancer status, the backend status and the timings. If the balancer status is 502 and the backend status is empty, the backend never answered. If both are present, the backend itself returned the error. Logs on both sides, joined by a request id, remove the guesswork. Keep alive If the balancer reuses a connection that the backend has already closed because of its own idle timeout, the next request fails with a 502 at random. The rule is that the backend idle timeout must be longer than the balancer idle timeout. Check both settings and make the application server follow the rule. Health and draining During a deployment, a pod that is stopped while still in the target list receives requests that fail. Use readiness checks, a preStop delay so that the endpoint is removed first, and connection draining on the balancer. Ask whether the errors line up with deployment times. Capacity A backend that reaches its worker or connection limit queues requests, and the balancer times out. Look at saturation: active connections, thread pool use and queue length. Scale out, add a limit that fails fast with a clear 503 or find the slow dependency that holds the workers. Scenario: intermittent 502, 503 and 504 behind a load balancer Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Backend pods; 3, Backend pods to Dependency. Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Outside party What it does: Request id. If it fails or is weak: Retries. Load balancer Service What it does: Idle timeout. If it fails or is weak: Reuse closed. Backend pods Service What it does: Keep alive. If it fails or is weak: Closes early. Dependency Service What it does: Slow call. If it fails or is weak: Holds workers. Incident: Random 502s every few minutes Incident About half a percent of requests returned 502 with no pattern in the application logs. $ grep &#x27; 502 &#x27; lb-access.log | head -1 2026-11-12T10:14:03Z lb-1 502 - 0.001 backend=10.2.4.17:8080 error=connection closed $ grep -E \"keepalive_timeout|idle_timeout\" app.conf lb.conf app.conf: keepalive_timeout 5s; lb.conf: idle_timeout 60s; (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The application server closed idle connections after five seconds, while the balancer kept them for sixty. Whenever a request was sent on a connection that had just been closed, the balancer logged a 502 with no backend status. The team set the application keep alive to seventy five seconds, longer than the balancer, and the errors stopped. They added the rule to the service template. Common mistakes Backend idle timeout shorter than the balancer Stopping pods before they leave the target list No request id across hops Retrying without limits Treating every 5xx as an application bug"},"troubleshooting-scenarios/scenario-slower-after-a-deploy-pools-gc-locks-and-neighbours":{"html":"\u003cp>Latency regressions are rarely a single slow line. They are usually a queue somewhere that filled. The job is to find the queue by looking at where time is spent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define the regression with percentiles and a baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where the time goes: traces and breakdowns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connection pools and thread pools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Garbage collection and memory behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locks, caches and noisy neighbours\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"define-it\">Define it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare the same endpoint before and after, at the same traffic level, using p50, p95 and p99. A change only in the tail points to queuing or pauses. A change in the median points to more work per request. Check traffic first, because a surge can look like a regression.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-time-goes\">Where time goes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a trace for a slow request and read the spans: time in the application, in each dependency and waiting. If there is a gap between spans, the request was waiting for a thread, a connection or the scheduler. Compare a slow and a fast trace side by side, and look for the new call or the call that became slower.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pools\">Pools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A connection pool or thread pool that is too small makes requests wait, and one that is too large overloads the database. Look at pool wait time, active and idle counts and rejected requests. A new feature that holds a connection longer, or a leak that never returns one, drains the pool and latency rises with load.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"garbage-collection\">Garbage collection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In managed runtimes, long or frequent pauses add to the tail. Check pause time and frequency, heap size against the container limit, and allocation rate. A deployment that increased object creation or reduced the heap can move the application into constant collection. Fix allocation or sizing, not the symptom.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks-caches-and-neighbours\">Locks, caches and neighbours\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A new lock in a hot path serialises requests. A cache key change can drop the hit rate to near zero and send all load to the database. A noisy neighbour on the same node can take CPU or disk, which shows as throttling or steal time. Check each with a metric before changing code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"base&gt;trace;trace&gt;pool;pool&gt;dep\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scenario: slower after a deploy: pools, garbage collection, locks and neighbours\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"base\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Before and after\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trace\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trace breakdown\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pools and GC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dep\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dependencies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Before and after to Trace breakdown; 2, Trace breakdown to Pools and GC; 3, Pools and GC to Dependencies.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"base\">\u003cdt>Before and after \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Same traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Surge.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trace\">\u003cdt>Trace breakdown \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Where time goes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Gaps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>Pools and GC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Waiting.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Exhausted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dep\">\u003cdt>Dependencies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cache and DB.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Miss.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-p99-tripled-after-a-harmless-change\">Incident: p99 tripled after a harmless change\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A release that changed only a cache key format made the 99th percentile latency three times worse.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:9090/metrics | grep -E &quot;cache_(hits|misses)_total&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:9090/metrics | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cache_(hits|misses)_total&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache_hits_total 1204\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache_misses_total 98233\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new key format did not match the keys already stored, and a bug added a random part to each key, so almost every lookup missed and went to the database. The database became the queue, and the tail latency rose. The team fixed the key, warmed the cache before the next release and added hit rate to the deployment checks and an alert on a sudden drop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Comparing at different traffic levels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looking only at averages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring pool wait time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing code before measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking cache hit rate after key changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"define-it","text":"Define it"},{"id":"where-time-goes","text":"Where time goes"},{"id":"pools","text":"Pools"},{"id":"garbage-collection","text":"Garbage collection"},{"id":"locks-caches-and-neighbours","text":"Locks, caches and neighbours"},{"id":"incident-p99-tripled-after-a-harmless-change","text":"Incident: p99 tripled after a harmless change"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenTelemetry traces","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"},{"t":"The USE method","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html"},{"t":"SRE Workbook: debugging","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/effective-troubleshooting/"}],"text":"Latency regressions are rarely a single slow line. They are usually a queue somewhere that filled. The job is to find the queue by looking at where time is spent. What you will learn Define the regression with percentiles and a baseline Where the time goes: traces and breakdowns Connection pools and thread pools Garbage collection and memory behaviour Locks, caches and noisy neighbours Define it Compare the same endpoint before and after, at the same traffic level, using p50, p95 and p99. A change only in the tail points to queuing or pauses. A change in the median points to more work per request. Check traffic first, because a surge can look like a regression. Where time goes Use a trace for a slow request and read the spans: time in the application, in each dependency and waiting. If there is a gap between spans, the request was waiting for a thread, a connection or the scheduler. Compare a slow and a fast trace side by side, and look for the new call or the call that became slower. Pools A connection pool or thread pool that is too small makes requests wait, and one that is too large overloads the database. Look at pool wait time, active and idle counts and rejected requests. A new feature that holds a connection longer, or a leak that never returns one, drains the pool and latency rises with load. Garbage collection In managed runtimes, long or frequent pauses add to the tail. Check pause time and frequency, heap size against the container limit, and allocation rate. A deployment that increased object creation or reduced the heap can move the application into constant collection. Fix allocation or sizing, not the symptom. Locks, caches and neighbours A new lock in a hot path serialises requests. A cache key change can drop the hit rate to near zero and send all load to the database. A noisy neighbour on the same node can take CPU or disk, which shows as throttling or steal time. Check each with a metric before changing code. Scenario: slower after a deploy: pools, garbage collection, locks and neighbours Flow in order: 1, Before and after to Trace breakdown; 2, Trace breakdown to Pools and GC; 3, Pools and GC to Dependencies. Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Before and after Observability What it does: Same traffic. If it fails or is weak: Surge. Trace breakdown Observability What it does: Where time goes. If it fails or is weak: Gaps. Pools and GC Step What it does: Waiting. If it fails or is weak: Exhausted. Dependencies Data store What it does: Cache and DB. If it fails or is weak: Miss. Incident: p99 tripled after a harmless change Incident A release that changed only a cache key format made the 99th percentile latency three times worse. $ curl -s localhost:9090/metrics | grep -E \"cache_(hits|misses)_total\" cache_hits_total 1204 cache_misses_total 98233 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The new key format did not match the keys already stored, and a bug added a random part to each key, so almost every lookup missed and went to the database. The database became the queue, and the tail latency rose. The team fixed the key, warmed the cache before the next release and added hit rate to the deployment checks and an alert on a sudden drop. Common mistakes Comparing at different traffic levels Looking only at averages Ignoring pool wait time Changing code before measuring Not checking cache hit rate after key changes"},"troubleshooting-scenarios/scenario-cloud-api-failures-access-denied-quotas-and-throttling":{"html":"\u003cp>Many outages are a control plane saying no. The message is usually precise, and the audit log tells you who asked for what.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading an access denied properly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quotas, limits and capacity errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Throttling and retry with backoff\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Eventual consistency after creating resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the audit log to find the call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-denied\">Access denied\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An access denied can come from the identity policy, a resource policy, a permission boundary, an organisation level deny, a missing condition such as region or tag, or an expired credential. The error often names the action and the resource. Use the policy simulator or the provider troubleshooting tool, and find which layer denied it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quotas-and-capacity\">Quotas and capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Accounts have limits on instances, addresses, rules and API rates, and these are per region. An error such as limit exceeded means a quota, while insufficient capacity means the provider has no more of that type in that zone right now. Request a quota increase early, and design to try another type or zone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"throttling\">Throttling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud APIs rate limit callers and return errors such as too many requests. A loop that calls list or describe for every item can hit limits. Add exponential backoff with jitter, cache results, use batch calls and spread work. Do not retry instantly in a tight loop, as that makes the throttling worse for everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"eventual-consistency\">Eventual consistency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some services need a short time before a new resource or policy is visible everywhere. A script that creates a role and uses it at once may fail with not found or no permission, and then work a minute later. Wait and retry with a limit, or use the wait operations that the tools provide.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-log\">Audit log\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Search the audit log for the failing call around the time of the error. It shows the caller identity, the action, the source address and the error code. This turns a vague failure into a precise one, and it also shows unexpected callers, which matters for security.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"call&gt;pol;pol&gt;lim;lim&gt;aud\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Scenario: cloud API failures: access denied, quotas and throttling\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"call\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API caller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lim\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Quotas and rates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, API caller to Policies; 2, Policies to Quotas and rates; 3, Quotas and rates to Audit log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"call\">\u003cdt>API caller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expired.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allow or deny.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hidden deny.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lim\">\u003cdt>Quotas and rates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Per region.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Exceeded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who and what.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not read.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-deploy-that-failed-only-in-one-region\">Incident: The deploy that failed only in one region\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline deployed successfully in two regions and failed in the third with an authorisation error.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 1 --query &quot;Events[0].CloudTrailEvent&quot; --output text | head -c 200\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 1 --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Events[0].CloudTrailEvent&quot;\u003c/span> --output text | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;errorCode&quot;:&quot;UnauthorizedOperation&quot;,&quot;errorMessage&quot;:&quot;explicit deny in a service control policy&quot;,&quot;awsRegion&quot;:&quot;eu-south-2&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The audit event named an explicit deny in an organisation policy that allowed only approved regions, and the third region was not on the list. The pipeline role itself was correct, so editing it would never have helped. The team asked the platform owners to approve the region through the normal process, and added the region list to the pipeline checks so that the error appears at plan time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing the identity policy when a higher layer denies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying instantly after throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requesting quota increases only during an incident\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a new resource is usable at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not looking at the audit log\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"access-denied","text":"Access denied"},{"id":"quotas-and-capacity","text":"Quotas and capacity"},{"id":"throttling","text":"Throttling"},{"id":"eventual-consistency","text":"Eventual consistency"},{"id":"audit-log","text":"Audit log"},{"id":"incident-the-deploy-that-failed-only-in-one-region","text":"Incident: The deploy that failed only in one region"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS CloudTrail user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html"},{"t":"AWS service quotas","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/servicequotas/latest/userguide/intro.html"},{"t":"Retry with backoff and jitter","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"}],"text":"Many outages are a control plane saying no. The message is usually precise, and the audit log tells you who asked for what. What you will learn Reading an access denied properly Quotas, limits and capacity errors Throttling and retry with backoff Eventual consistency after creating resources Using the audit log to find the call Access denied An access denied can come from the identity policy, a resource policy, a permission boundary, an organisation level deny, a missing condition such as region or tag, or an expired credential. The error often names the action and the resource. Use the policy simulator or the provider troubleshooting tool, and find which layer denied it. Quotas and capacity Accounts have limits on instances, addresses, rules and API rates, and these are per region. An error such as limit exceeded means a quota, while insufficient capacity means the provider has no more of that type in that zone right now. Request a quota increase early, and design to try another type or zone. Throttling Cloud APIs rate limit callers and return errors such as too many requests. A loop that calls list or describe for every item can hit limits. Add exponential backoff with jitter, cache results, use batch calls and spread work. Do not retry instantly in a tight loop, as that makes the throttling worse for everyone. Eventual consistency Some services need a short time before a new resource or policy is visible everywhere. A script that creates a role and uses it at once may fail with not found or no permission, and then work a minute later. Wait and retry with a limit, or use the wait operations that the tools provide. Audit log Search the audit log for the failing call around the time of the error. It shows the caller identity, the action, the source address and the error code. This turns a vague failure into a precise one, and it also shows unexpected callers, which matters for security. Scenario: cloud API failures: access denied, quotas and throttling Flow in order: 1, API caller to Policies; 2, Policies to Quotas and rates; 3, Quotas and rates to Audit log. Service Security Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. API caller Service What it does: Identity. If it fails or is weak: Expired. Policies Security What it does: Allow or deny. If it fails or is weak: Hidden deny. Quotas and rates Step What it does: Per region. If it fails or is weak: Exceeded. Audit log Observability What it does: Who and what. If it fails or is weak: Not read. Incident: The deploy that failed only in one region Incident A pipeline deployed successfully in two regions and failed in the third with an authorisation error. $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 1 --query \"Events[0].CloudTrailEvent\" --output text | head -c 200 {\"errorCode\":\"UnauthorizedOperation\",\"errorMessage\":\"explicit deny in a service control policy\",\"awsRegion\":\"eu-south-2\"} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The audit event named an explicit deny in an organisation policy that allowed only approved regions, and the third region was not on the list. The pipeline role itself was correct, so editing it would never have helped. The team asked the platform owners to approve the region through the normal process, and added the region list to the pipeline checks so that the error appears at plan time. Common mistakes Editing the identity policy when a higher layer denies Retrying instantly after throttling Requesting quota increases only during an incident Assuming a new resource is usable at once Not looking at the audit log"}});